## The console

::: footer
Programming with R: Basics
:::

::: {style="font-size: 30px;"}
Type this computation `100*23` on the console and press enter... (the `>` symbol means where the console is expecting you to write a command)
:::

```         
> 100*23
```

```{r}
#| echo: false
100*23
```

::: {style="font-size: 30px;"}
You can do this too on a R script, all the outputs of the code you executive will be shown on the console.

Try this one on your script...
:::

```{r}
#| echo: true
1000:1020
```

::: {style="font-size: 30px;"}
Or use it for solving computations...
:::

```{r}
#| echo: true
(7 + 7) / 7 * 2 - 4
```

## Using Scripts

::: footer
Programming with R: Basics
:::

In RStudio we can work with multiple file types. The most common file will be the R script. An script for R will use the **.R** extension in the file name, for example *myscript.R*

For creating an script you can go to new file icon on the left corner in RStudio and select R Script option. 

![](img/script_creation.png){width="800" style="margin-left:100px;"}

## Using Scripts

::: {style="font-size: 40px;"}
Your new script will appear on the editor tab, you can have multiple scripts open. Inside the script you can write code and comments, all needs to be saved each time so it can be executed later. For saving you can use save command: `CTRL+S` (Windows) or `command+S` (macOS), or simply selecting save icon. 

:::

::: {layout-ncol=2}

![Unsaved script name color is red](img/script_unsaved.png){width="400" style="margin-left:100px;"}

![Saved script name is black](img/script_saved.png){width="400" style="margin-left:100px;"}

:::

## Writing comments

::: footer
Programming with R: Basics
:::

Commenting is important to understand your code, use `#` on your scripts to add comments...

```{r, echo=TRUE}
# This is a comment
# Sum all numbers from 1 to 100
sum(1:100)
```

::: callout-tip
Everyone codes differently and comments helps understanding code written from different programmers, and can always help you understand your own code after some time... **Take time to add comments to your code always!**
:::

## Variables

::: footer
Programming with R: Basics
:::

::: {style="font-size: 30px;"}
We will need to keep a value or the result of an operation, for doing that, we need to store them on the memory with an identifier... a **variable**.
:::

```{r, echo=TRUE}
# This will create a variable with value 10
